Lexicographically Smallest Subsequence of Distinct Characters (Java)

Problem Statement

Given a string "s", return the lexicographically smallest subsequence of "s" that contains all the distinct characters exactly once.

A subsequence is formed by deleting zero or more characters without changing the order of the remaining characters.

Example 1

Input: s = "bcabc"
Output: "abc"

Example 2

Input: s = "cbacdcbc"
Output: "acdb"

---

Approach (Greedy + Monotonic Stack)

The idea is to build the answer greedily while ensuring every distinct character appears exactly once.

Step 1: Store the Last Occurrence

First, store the last index of every character.

Example:

s = "cbacdcbc"

Character : Last Index
c : 7
b : 6
a : 2
d : 4

This helps us decide whether a character can be removed from the current answer because it will appear again later.

Step 2: Build the Answer

Maintain:

- "last[]" → Last occurrence of every character.
- "used[]" → Checks whether a character is already included.
- "StringBuilder" → Works as a stack.

For each character:

1. If it is already used, skip it.

2. Otherwise, while:
   
   - Stack is not empty.
   - Top character is greater than the current character.
   - Top character appears again later.
   
   Remove the top character from the stack.

3. Add the current character to the stack.

This guarantees the lexicographically smallest valid subsequence.

---

Dry Run

s = "cbacdcbc"

Read c -> c
Read b -> remove c, push b -> b
Read a -> remove b, push a -> a
Read c -> ac
Read d -> acd
Read c -> already used
Read b -> acdb
Read c -> already used

Answer = acdb

---

Complexity Analysis

- Time Complexity: "O(n)"
- Space Complexity: "O(1)" (26 lowercase letters)

---

Java Solution

class Solution {
    public String smallestSubsequence(String s) {
        int[] last = new int[26];
        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            last[s.charAt(i) - 'a'] = i;
        }

        boolean[] used = new boolean[26];
        StringBuilder st = new StringBuilder();

        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            char c = s.charAt(i);

            if (used[c - 'a']) {
                continue;
            }

            while (st.length() > 0 &&
                   st.charAt(st.length() - 1) > c &&
                   last[st.charAt(st.length() - 1) - 'a'] > i) {
                used[st.charAt(st.length() - 1) - 'a'] = false;
                st.deleteCharAt(st.length() - 1);
            }

            st.append(c);
            used[c - 'a'] = true;
        }

        return st.toString();
    }
}
